# Notes notebook production adapter

This isolated adapter is ready for a parent-owned integration review. No production application, host, requirement registry or prior receipt was changed.

The candidate notebook runtime and its tests are byte-identical to the frozen 283-artifact notebook stage. The only app-file adaptations are five test-oracle path substitutions and README links to the actual preserved notice and provenance paths. `adaptations.json` binds the before and after identities. The copied host/storage/catalog/public files are private test infrastructure; they are not proposed shared-file replacements.

The proposed application-relative study is `products/rea-studio/apps/notes/results/notebooks-followup/stage/`. Every original stage artifact is preserved byte-for-byte with an additional `.snapshot` suffix, including old failures, run sources, logs, browser observations and original license/source records. `preservation-manifest.json` maps all 283 original paths and the separately preserved original strict manifest. The runnable test oracle reads this frozen app-relative source, so the normal tests run offline and emit observations to stdout instead of writing app results.

The exact run under `runs/2026-10-12T22-39-19.649Z` passed 28/28 tests plus the two unchanged root-authored held-persistence scenarios. The actual copied host passed notebook operations through HTTP and two cold restarts with preserved identities, hierarchy, attachments and JEX output. A separate private host served client/Markdown modules and returned 404 for the application server, notebook implementation, test oracle and study sources. Runtime import review found no original helper execution in the app. Full-browser interaction was not repeated here; the original stage's browser evidence remains historical and unchanged.

Original comparison accounting remains finite: 20 nesting comparisons, four path comparisons and two candidate-versus-original move comparisons, with two additional standalone original move-contract executions. Three original helper contexts execute four released Folder/Note method slices with fixed clocks and in-memory load/save stubs; the reserved-ID source helper is also authenticated. No original Joplin database, desktop executable, synchronization or GUI is executed. The old 22-check JEX serializer receipt remains bound to its separate historical source epoch and is not rebased onto this adapter.

The original source is released Desktop 3.6.14, commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`. The exact upstream LICENSE, package declarations, copyright notices, ancestor-license responses and the source-assisted notice are preserved. They declare AGPL-3.0-or-later for the selected source; this review is not a broader legal or distribution-license determination. Candidate source-assisted behavior does not establish complete product parity, security or performance superiority. Shared/trash/conflict organization, complete importer/database lifecycle, sharing/synchronization, autosave/crash recovery and full search/accessibility behavior remain outside the slice.

`record-adapter.mjs` binds runnable files and all inert study artifacts before and after the run. It caps child stdout/stderr and imposes a 180-second child deadline; these are recorder bounds, not bootstrap/fingerprint I/O deadlines or guaranteed Windows descendant isolation. Synthetic host data and normal test data use owned temporary folders. The exact initial manifest-identity typo that prevented the first preparation attempt before any folder/test/source mutation is preserved under `failures/prepare-manifest-literal/`.

Integration should copy only the proposed Notes app changes and its `results/notebooks-followup/` preservation, retaining old results. Parent-owned shared files remain outside this adapter's authorized changes.
